parts.text:
User chose "技能砖+血砖都震" — skill bricks AND health bricks (red/green HP bricks in HP mode) all vibrate.

So vibration triggers:
1. `triggerSkill()` — all skill brick hits (kind > 0): 变快/增长/分身 (main ball + blue balls)
2. HP-mode red brick hit (hp--) and green brick hit (hp++) — the blood bricks

Looking at the brick hit code structure in tick():
```js
var kindHit = this[this._bk[i]]
if (kindHit > 0) {
  this.score += 10
  this.triggerSkill(kindHit, ...)
  ...
} else if (this.isHp && this[this._bc[i]] === '#ff3333') {
  this.hp--
  ...
} else if (this.isHp && this[this._bc[i]] === '#33cc33') {
  if (this.hp < 5) this.hp++
} else {
  this.score += 10
  ...
}
```

So for blood bricks: add vibrate in the two isHp branches (red and green). Or simpler: create a helper `buzz()` and call it in triggerSkill + the two HP branches.

Note: blue balls hitting red/green bricks — the blue ball loop's brick hit does `this.score += 5` and `triggerSkill` only if kindL > 0. Blue balls don't have red/green HP semantics (looking at the blue ball loop: it just scores +5 regardless of color, and triggers skill if kind>0). So blood brick vibration only in main ball loop — matches game logic.

Now the plan. Ball 24px too.

Plan file: plans dir. Then present summary and wait for confirmation to execute (plan mode!).

Changes:
1. manifest.json: add `{ "name": "system.vibrator" }`
2. game.ux:
   - `import vibrator from '@system.vibrator'`
   - helper `buzz()`:
 ```js
   buzz() {
     try {
       if (vibrator.getSystemDefaultMode && vibrator.getSystemDefaultMode() === 0) return
     } catch (e) {}
     try { vibrator.vibrate({ mode: 'short' }) } catch (e) {}
   }
   ```
   Hmm, getSystemDefaultMode — per docs it returns 0/1/2 synchronously. Guard if system setting is off (0). But maybe simpler to skip that check — vibrate respects system settings usually. But the user said "震动时长不要持续太久" — only duration matters. Adding getSystemDefaultMode guard is a nice touch but adds complexity. Actually if user has vibration off system-wide, calling vibrate might still vibrate (annoying). The guard is worth it. But is getSystemDefaultMode sync? Docs say returns Number — sync. OK include with try/catch fallback to just vibrate.

   Actually simpler: just call vibrate({mode:'short'}) in try/catch. The system may respect its own settings. But to be safe and respectful, include the getSystemDefaultMode check. Keep it simple:
   ```js
   buzz() {
     try {
       if (typeof vibrator.getSystemDefaultMode === 'function' && vibrator.getSystemDefaultMode() === 0) return
     } catch (e) {}
     try { vibrator.vibrate({ mode: 'short' }) } catch (e) {}
   }
   ```
   - Call `this.buzz()` at top of `triggerSkill()`
   - Call `this.buzz()` in the two HP branches (red hp--, green hp++)
3. Ball size: `this.ballS = 26` → `24`; CSS .ball/.ball-blue 26px→24px, border-radius 13px→12px

Verify: node --check + clone build + grep.

Write plan file now.

